WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:07.090
The choice of the learning rate alpha will have a huge impact on the efficiency of your

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:13.170
implementation of gradient descent, and if alpha, the learning rate, is chosen poorly,

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.850
gradient descent may not even work at all.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.730
In this video, let's take a deeper look at the learning rate.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:24.930
This will also help you choose better learning rates for your implementations of gradient

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:25.930
descent.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:29.970
So, here again is the gradient descent rule.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:35.870
W is updated to be W minus the learning rate alpha times the derivative term.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:39.890
To learn more about what the learning rate alpha is doing, let's see what could happen

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:45.330
if the learning rate alpha is either too small or if it is too large.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:51.130
For the case where the learning rate is too small, here's a graph where the horizontal

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:56.210
axis is W and the vertical axis is the cost J.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:01.410
And here's a graph of the function J of W.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.210
Let's start gradient descent at this point here.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:11.250
If the learning rate is too small, then what happens is that you multiply your derivative

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.290
term by some really really really small number.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:23.470
So you're going to be multiplying by a number alpha that's really small, like 0.0000001.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:28.470
And so you end up taking a very small baby step like that.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:32.670
Then from this point, you're going to take another, you know, tiny tiny little baby

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.230
step.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:40.470
But because the learning rate is so small, the second step is also just miniscule.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:45.870
The outcome of this process is that you do end up decreasing the cost J, but incredibly

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.430
slowly.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:53.230
So here's another step, and another step, another tiny step, until you finally approach

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.790
the minimum.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:59.550
But as you may notice, you're going to need a lot of steps to get to the minimum.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:06.350
So to summarize, if the learning rate is too small, then gradient descent will work.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:10.390
But it will be slow, it will take a very long time because it's going to take these

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:15.750
tiny tiny baby steps, and it's going to need a lot of steps before it gets anywhere close

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.870
to the minimum.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.990
Now let's look at a different case.

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:23.550
What happens if the learning rate is too large?

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.910
Here's another graph of the cost function.

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:32.470
And let's say we start gradient descent with W at this value here.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:37.550
So it's actually already pretty close to the minimum.

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.510
So the derivative points to the right.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:49.310
But if the learning rate is too large, then you update W by a giant step to be all the

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.830
way over here.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:56.430
And that's this point here on the function J.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:02.070
So you move from this point on the left all the way to this point on the right.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.790
And now the cost has actually gotten worse.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:08.710
It has increased because it has started out at this value here.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:13.790
And after one step, it actually increased to this value here.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:19.590
Now the derivative at this new point says to decrease W.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:25.390
But when the learning rate is too big, then you may take a huge step going from here all

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:27.910
the way out here.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.910
So now you've gotten to this point here.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:35.350
And again, if the learning rate is too big, then you take another huge step in the next

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.230
iteration and way overshoot the minimum again.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.510
So now you're at this point on the right.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:46.670
And one more time, you do another update and end up all the way here.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:51.390
And so you're now at this point here.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:57.330
So as you may notice, you're actually getting further and further away from the minimum.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:03.790
So if the learning rate is too large, then gradient descent may overshoot and may never

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.030
reach the minimum.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:14.930
And another way to say that is that gradient descent may fail to converge and may even diverge.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:19.270
So here's another question you may be wondering.

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:25.990
One of your parameter W is already at this point here, so that your cost J is already

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:29.550
at a local minimum.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:35.870
What do you think one step of gradient descent will do if you've already reached a minimum?

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:39.070
So this is a tricky one.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:43.790
When I was first learning this stuff, it actually took me a long time to figure it out.

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:46.070
But let's work through this together.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:50.310
Let's suppose you have some cost function J.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:54.830
And the one you see here isn't a squared error cost function.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:05:01.550
And this cost function has two local minima corresponding to two values that you see

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:02.550
here.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:08.470
Now, let's suppose that after some number of steps of gradient descent, your parameter

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:13.590
W is over here, say equal to 5.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:16.590
And so this is the current value of W.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:21.510
This means that you're at this point on the cost function J, and that happens to be

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.830
a local minimum.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:29.230
Turns out if you draw a tangent to the function at this point, the slope of this line is

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:30.790
0.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:37.550
And thus, the derivative term here is equal to 0 for the current value of W.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:44.710
And so your gradient descent update becomes W is updated to W minus the learning rate times

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:50.950
0, where here that's because the derivative term is equal to 0.

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:57.150
And this is the same as saying let's set W to be equal to W.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:02.790
So this means that if you're already at the local minimum, gradient descent leaves

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:08.830
W unchanged because it just updates the new value of W to be the exact same old value

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:10.870
of W.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:18.870
So concretely, let's say if the current value of W is 5, and alpha is 0.1, after

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:29.110
one iteration, you update W as W minus alpha times 0, and it is still equal to 5.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:35.070
So if your parameters have already brought you to a local minimum, then further gradient

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.750
descent steps do absolutely nothing.

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:41.790
It doesn't change the parameters, which is what you want, because it keeps the solution

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.310
at that local minimum.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:49.910
This also explains why gradient descent can reach a local minimum even with a fixed learning

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:50.910
rate alpha.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.310
Here's what I mean.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:57.390
To illustrate this, let's look at another example.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:02.590
Here's the cost function J of W that we want to minimize.

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:07.470
Let's initialize gradient descent up here at this point.

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:14.150
If we take one update step, maybe it'll take us to that point.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:20.750
And because this derivative is pretty large, gradient descent takes a relatively big step,

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:21.750
right?

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:26.870
Now, we're at this second point, where we take another step.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:31.630
And you may notice that the slope is not as deep as it was at the first point, so

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:33.990
the derivative isn't as large.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:39.550
And so the next update step will not be as large as that first step.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:46.330
Now, we're at this third point here, and the derivative is smaller than it was at the

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:51.050
previous step, and we'll take an even smaller step.

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:57.370
As we approach the minimum, the derivative gets closer and closer to zero, so as we

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:03.710
run gradient descent, eventually we're taking very small steps until you finally reach

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:05.210
a local minimum.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:11.390
So just to recap, as we get nearer a local minimum, gradient descent will automatically

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:13.470
take smaller steps.

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:18.430
And that's because as we approach the local minimum, the derivative automatically gets

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:25.030
smaller, and that means the update steps also automatically get smaller even if the

00:08:25.030 --> 00:08:28.790
learning rate alpha is kept at some fixed value.

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:31.810
So that's the gradient descent algorithm.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:36.970
You can use it to try to minimize any cost function j, not just the mean squared

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:41.630
error cost function that we're using for linear regression.

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:47.570
In the next video, we're going to take the function j and set that back to be exactly

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:52.170
the linear regression model's cost function, the mean squared error cost function

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:54.690
that we had come up with earlier.

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:59.090
And putting together gradient descent with this cost function, that will give you your

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:02.970
first learning algorithm, the linear regression algorithm.
